---
source: packages/tier1/plot/tests/printing.rs
expression: all
---
== the_plot_maps_no_y
error[Z0401]: `lines` places each mark by its `x` and its `y`, but the plot maps no variable to `y`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:17
  |
4 | m.plot(x: year).lines()
  |                 ^^^^^ needs both `x` and `y`
  |
  = help: map both where the plot starts, as in `plot(x: educ, y: wage)`

== neither_maps_x
error[Z0401]: `lines` places each mark by its `x` and its `y`, but neither the plot nor this layer maps a variable to `x`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:10
  |
4 | m.plot().lines(y: wage)
  |          ^^^^^ needs both `x` and `y`
  |
  = help: name it in the layer, as in `lines(x: educ, y: wage)`, or where the plot starts, as in `plot(x: educ, y: wage)`

== no_such_variable
error[Z0101]: no variable `wag` in `m`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:35
  |
4 | m.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(y: wag)
  |                                   ^^^ not a variable of m
  |
  = help: did you mean `wage`? If you meant a category level, write it in quotes: `"wage"`

== not_a_number
error[Z0401]: the plot places `wage` on a linear scale along `y`, but `industry`, which `lines` maps to `y`, is a Category, which is placed as categories
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:35
  |
4 | w.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(y: industry)
  |                                   ^^^^^^^^ `industry` is of another kind
  |
  = help: the layers of a plot share one axis, so what is mapped to `y` must all be categories (a Str or a Category), all be numbers or all be dates (a Date or a DateTime); `category(v)` makes categories of a number

== no_such_group
error[Z0101]: no variable `unionn` in `u`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:39
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(group: unionn)
  |                                       ^^^^^^ not a variable of u
  |
  = help: did you mean `union`? If you meant a category level, write it in quotes: `"union"`

== a_group_in_quotes
error[Z0401]: `group` of `lines` must be a Var, but this is a Str
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:39
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(group: "union")
  |                                       ^^^^^^^ a Str
  |
  = help: name a variable of the frame, without quotes

== a_group_computed
error[Z0401]: `group:` of `lines` takes a variable of the frame, not an expression
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:39
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(group: year > 2015)
  |                                       ^^^^^^^^^^^ an expression
  |
  = help: compute it first on the frame, with `add(v = year > 2015)`, and name the new variable: `.lines(group: v)`

== a_colour_by_a_number
error[Z0401]: `lines` draws each line in one colour, so `color` must show categories, but `wage` is a Float without value labels, which is shown on a continuous scale
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:39
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(color: wage)
  |                                       ^^^^ a colour for each row, not for each line
  |
  = help: colour the lines by a variable of categories, as in `lines(color: union)`, or set one colour for this layer, as in `lines(color: "black")`

== a_colour_by_a_number_of_the_plot
error[Z0401]: `lines` draws each line in one colour, so `color` must show categories, but `wage` is a Float without value labels, which is shown on a continuous scale
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:48
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage, color: wage).points().lines()
  |                                                ^^^^^ a colour for each row, not for each line
  |
  = help: colour the lines by a variable of categories, as in `lines(color: union)`, or set one colour for this layer, as in `lines(color: "black")`

== not_a_colour
error[Z0401]: `color:` of `lines` is "blu", which is not the name of a colour
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:39
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(color: "blu")
  |                                       ^^^^^ not a colour
  |
  = help: did you mean "blue"?

== a_type_by_a_number
error[Z0401]: `lines` gives each category a type of line, so `wage` must be a Bool, Str or Category variable, or a number with value labels, but it is a Float without value labels
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:42
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(linetype: wage)
  |                                          ^^^^ not categories
  |
  = help: a type of line shows a category; make the number categories first, as in `u.add(wage = category(wage))`

== not_a_type
error[Z0401]: `linetype:` of `lines` is "dash", which is not a type of line
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:42
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(linetype: "dash")
  |                                          ^^^^^^ not a type of line
  |
  = help: a type of line is one of solid, dashed, dotted, dash_dot, long_dash and dash_dot_dot

== a_type_without_quotes
error[Z0101]: no variable `dashed` in `u`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:42
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(linetype: dashed)
  |                                          ^^^^^^ not a variable of u
  |
  = help: `dashed` is a type of line: to draw every line of the layer that way, write it in quotation marks, `linetype: "dashed"`

== a_variable_in_quotes_for_a_type
error[Z0401]: `linetype:` of `lines` is "union", which is not a type of line
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:42
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(linetype: "union")
  |                                          ^^^^^^^ not a type of line
  |
  = help: a string sets one type for every line; to give each category of `union` a type, write it without quotation marks, `linetype: union`

== a_type_computed
error[Z0401]: `linetype:` of `lines` takes a variable of the frame or a string, not an expression
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:42
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(linetype: union == 1)
  |                                          ^^^^^^^^^^ an expression
  |
  = help: compute it first on the frame, with `add(v = union == 1)`, and name the new variable: `.lines(linetype: v)`

== no_width
error[Z0401]: `width:` of `lines` is the width of a line in points of 1/72 inch, above 0, but this is 0
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:39
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(width: 0)
  |                                       ^ not a width
  |
  = help: write a width such as 0.5 or 2; without `width:` a line is 1 wide

== a_negative_width
error[Z0401]: `width:` of `lines` is the width of a line in points of 1/72 inch, above 0, but this is -1
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:39
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(width: -1)
  |                                       ^^ not a width
  |
  = help: write a width such as 0.5 or 2; without `width:` a line is 1 wide

== a_width_in_words
error[Z0401]: `width` of `lines` must be a Float, but this is a Str
 --> scripts/main.zeff:4:39
  |
4 | u.plot(x: year, y: wage).lines(width: "thin")
  |                                       ^^^^^^ a Str
  |
  = help: usage: `p.lines(data: Frame, x: Var | Expr, y: Var | Expr, color: Var | Str, group: Var, linetype: Var | Str, width: Float = 1)`

